WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.450 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this past summer I landed the internship  of a lifetime no it wasn't that amazing  business development internship at  Airbnb or that super sexy product  management role at Amazon or even that  really cool secret project at unnamed  startup in stealth mode it was an  internship at Costco  yes Costco Wholesale the place that you  might remember as a concrete jungle  littered with oversized red shopping  carts or maybe the place that gives away  free food samples and is featured in  fresh off the boat or maybe you just  remember it as this and you might be  thinking to yourself why would a former  McKinsey consultant former VC investor  and current student at a Business School  in the heart of Silicon Valley want to  move to Issaquah Washington to work for  a large an old-school company like  Costco well as it turns out most of us  in this room have been chasing the next  sexy startup the next big unicorn but  what about the companies that have  endured and existed for years on end I  like to think of these as the Rhinos of  the business world they're definitely  not sexy they certainly have more  wrinkles they're slower moving they're  more methodical but they also have  thicker skin greater strength and more  wisdom and they may just be a little bit  more real than their mythological  counterparts indeed my summer opened my  eyes to just how wonderful these whiners  can be the lessons I learned in humility  in entrepreneurship and an impact are  ones that I will never forget muscle  number one working for a rhino is  humbling my alarm clock blares at 3:15  in the morning I rolled over I hit the  snooze button and I thought to myself oh  I could definitely use at least a couple  more hours of sleep  how could I possibly be this tired when  it was only my second day on the job I  made it out of bed clocked into the  warehouse right at 4:00 in the morning  grabbed a pallet jack and reported for  duty over the course of that morning my  hands got sore from moving what seemed  like endless amounts of campbell soup  from one pallet to the next my back  started to ache as I swept the floors  making sure that our aisles were  Showtime ready for our opening at 10:00  in the morning it was the hardest work I  had ever done and yet it only paid $20  an hour I often thought to myself what  am I doing here I couldn't help but  compare myself to my peers who I imagine  sitting at a desk building fancy models  in Excel or creating beautiful  PowerPoint presentations filled with  interesting strategies and yet here I  was I had a hard time seeing the value  of sweeping floors bringing customers up  at the cash registers and if making food  in the deli but then I met Joe Joe had  been with the company for the last 12  years you couldn't miss him with his six  foot two stature his baggy sweatpants  and the cap backwards Raiders cap on at  all times you always knew that he was on  shift because you could hear his hip-hop  and rap music blasting from his radio at  4:30 in the morning and as I got to know  him I learned that Joe didn't really  know what he wanted to do with his life  but he loved his job at Costco he told  me the company's been really good to me  it rewards me for something I'm actually  pretty good at so maybe one day I'll  apply to be a supervisor and I'll earn  my red vest and then maybe eventually I  could be a manager Joe's story was just  one of the many I heard the summer that  reminded me that my fellow warehouse  workers were each trying to achieve  their own version of the American Dream  they might not have gone to the same  schools as us or works for the same  companies as us but they too hit an  ambition to create a life of meaning for  themselves whether it was Nikki's love  of her 4:00 a.m. shift that enabled her  to pick up her daughter after school  or Alex's drive and determination to  work his way through college they were  all incredibly hard-working dedicated  and damn good at their jobs now that's  pretty humbling lesson number two you  can be entrepreneurial at a rhino I  remember I was out of breath because I  had been sprinting across a hundred and  fifty thousand square feet of concrete  warehouse floor a customer had asked me  if we still had the sleep science  mattress in stock in pursuit of her  question I first ran over to the  supervisor to ask her if she knew and  then I ran over to the stalker who  typically worked the furniture  department and then finally I ran back  to the office to check our a s400 system  only to come up with a lousy answer I'm  so sorry ma'am our system shows that we  have one but we don't know where it is I  thought to myself there must be a better  way to get this kind of information and  so I embarked on a journey to build them  a mobile app that would let them see  real-time inventory levels right on  their phones now let's not get carried  away Here I am by no means a coder but  I'm pretty good at using Google and so I  hacked together what we might generously  call a Minimum Viable Product when I  showed Tyler the merchandising manager  of the app he was stoked he asked if we  could augment it add more data to it  show more dashboards and pretty soon I  had a bunch of team members come up to  me to ask me how to download it and if  they could use it so I identified a  customer pain point I designed a  customer centric solution  I built an MVP and I pivoted on that  product and no I wasn't working for a  unicorn who says you have to work at one  to be an entrepreneur anyways lesson  number three rhinos shape communities  you've probably never heard of a town  called Fremont Nebraska but it's about  an hour outside of Omaha we were there  visiting Costco's construction site for  their poultry processing plant that had  just broken ground Fremont is pretty  desolate has one main  with a couple of stoplights that hang on  a wire a few mom-and-pop shops one bar  in town for all the locals the only  restaurant I recognized was at Buffalo  Wild Wings over the course of a three  night trip we ate there twice and I  remember sitting in the trailer on the  construction site and I was talking to  Jonathan the plant manager and he he was  explaining to me the org chart and I was  shocked at the team size they were  proposing as of April 2019 this plan  would create 800 new jobs in Fremont  alone I was stunned  I knew the Rhinos had impact whether it  was Costco's title as the largest  organic retailer in the US or the  largest seller of USDA choice in prime  beef I knew they were big but what I  hadn't fully realized is just how much  they impact local communities they  create employment they create  opportunity they create an economy a few  weeks ago a CEO verrano came to the GSB  and asked us don't you want to do  something more than just sell ad space  now maybe that's exactly what you want  to do or it's been your life's passion  to start a company to be a management  consultant to join a big tech firm I'm  not suggesting you shouldn't but what I  am suggesting is that you take a moment  to consider what you might be able to  achieve at a Rhino they're not for the  faint of heart they don't offer  free-flowing food or nap pods Starwood  points or shuttle buses but they do  offer a platform for humility for  Entrepreneurship and for impact so while  you're dreaming of what it might be like  to work at the next big unicorn that is  yet to exist don't forget about their  long-lost cousins the Rhinos I'm excited  to announce that I will be rejoining  Costco for their very official MBA  rotational program starting next fall  and of one don't get me wrong it  terrifies me that I'm not joining a  rocket ship or the big three or  fund-to-funds I'm scared that I'm  zagging when everyone else around us  seen  to be zigging but i know that for me  joining a rhino is my best possible  opportunity to change lives change  organizations and change the world  [Applause]  [Music]

